  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 215 PM CDT Sat Oct 31 2026\n\nCURRENTS & TONIGHT: Taking a look across the area, the dreary and \nwet conditions persists as lingering showers gradually lifting \nthrough the Highway-14 corridor this afternoon and areas of fog \ncontinue to erode. While a brief lull in activity could develop \nthrough the late afternoon hours, short-range guidance continues to \nshow a few additional showers developing by early evening \nmainly west of highway-81 as another wave swings through. Any \nresulting accumulations would be light at best. Lastly, with \novercast skies likely continuing through the evening hours; \ndecided to slightly increased overnight lows by blending in some\nof the CONSshort resulting in temperatures in the low to mid \n50s with the warmest conditions along our River Valleys. \n\nSUNDAY-TUESDAY: Not many forecast changes expected into Sunday as \nincreasing warm air advection (WAA) aloft keeps us locked into the \novercast and dreary conditions for most of the day. While most areas \nshould remain dry during the day, a few durnally-driven sprinkles to \nlight showers could develop in the lower cloud bases during the \nafternoon with limited accumulations. From here, the focus continues \nto be on the increased rain chances from Sunday night into Monday. \nLooking aloft, most of our rain chances (70%-100%) will be in \nresponse to a developing Colorado Low lifting from the Nebraska \nPanhandle into central and eastern SoDak throughout the day. \nWidespread rain is expected as increase lift in the warm air \nadvective wing leads to pockets of light to moderate rain for the \nfirst half of the day. From here, we'll likely lose mid-level \nsaturation during the afternoon hours as the dry slot progresses \nthrough the area. With this in mind, any lingering activity will \nbegin to dissipate as it lifts northeastwards. \n\nA few isolated thunderstorms can't be completely rule out across \nnorthwestern IA giving the low CAPE/high shear environment and \nincreased lift ahead of the triple point. However, confidence \ncontinues to decrease in any stronger activity mainly due to \nafternoon development across eastern Nebraska and northwestern \nMissouri effectively cutting off our moisture pool. Otherwise, \nadditional precipitation chances will return from Monday night into \nTuesday morning as the cold front swings through. Shifting gears to \namounts, ensemble guidance continues to show moderate to high \nconfidence (60%-80%) in at least 1 inch of QPF mainly along and west \nof I-29 with lesser amounts (0.50\" of an inch or less) elsewhere. \nWith trends continue to pull the highest amounts towards central SD \nwith the deformation zone/warm-front, wouldn't be surprised if QPF \namounts continue to trend downwards and northwestwards moving \nforward especially as more short-range guidance gets ingested into \nthe NBM QPF. Nonetheless, our flash flood risk continues to look low \nwith mainly concern being localized ponding in low-lying areas.\nLastly, with a tightening SPG on both Monday and Tuesday; expect \nsurface winds to strengthen with gust between 30-40 mph expected on \nboth day.\n\nWEDNESDAY ONWARDS: Heading into the extended period, an active \npattern continues aloft with medium-range guidance struggling to \nresolve the location of the upper-level low. While confidence is \nlow, can't rule out some wrap around showers throughout the day on \nWednesday especially east of I-29. Quieter conditions will return by \nThursday as mid-level ridging progresses through the area. This \nalong with the return mid-level warm air advection (WAA) should lead \nto slightly warmer temperatures heading into the weekend with highs \nincrease from the low to mid 50s by Wednesday into the low to mid \n60s by Thursday.",
